{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '170,215p' ../agent-source/raftpb/raft.proto\nsed -n '1950,2031p' ../agent-source/raft.go\nsed -n '110,153p' ../agent-source/rawnode.go\nsed -n '30,65p' ../agent-source/design.md\"","aggregated_output":"\n// ConfChangeSingle is an individual configuration change operation. Multiple\n// such operations can be carried out atomically via a ConfChangeV2.\nmessage ConfChangeSingle {\n\toptional ConfChangeType  type    = 1 [(gogoproto.nullable) = false];\n\toptional uint64          node_id = 2 [(gogoproto.nullable) = false, (gogoproto.customname) = \"NodeID\"];\n}\n\n// ConfChangeV2 messages initiate configuration changes. They support both the\n// simple \"one at a time\" membership change protocol and full Joint Consensus\n// allowing for arbitrary changes in membership.\n//\n// The supplied context is treated as an opaque payload and can be used to\n// attach an action on the state machine to the application of the config change\n// proposal. Note that contrary to Joint Consensus as outlined in the Raft\n// paper[1], configuration changes become active when they are *applied* to the\n// state machine (not when they are appended to the log).\n//\n// The simple protocol can be used whenever only a single change is made.\n//\n// Non-simple changes require the use of Joint Consensus, for which two\n// configuration changes are run. The first configuration change specifies the\n// desired changes and transitions the Raft group into the joint configuration,\n// in which quorum requires a majority of both the pre-changes and post-changes\n// configuration. Joint Consensus avoids entering fragile intermediate\n// configurations that could compromise survivability. For example, without the\n// use of Joint Consensus and running across three availability zones with a\n// replication factor of three, it is not possible to replace a voter without\n// entering an intermediate configuration that does not survive the outage of\n// one availability zone.\n//\n// The provided ConfChangeTransition specifies how (and whether) Joint Consensus\n// is used, and assigns the task of leaving the joint configuration either to\n// Raft or the application. Leaving the joint configuration is accomplished by\n// proposing a ConfChangeV2 with only and optionally the Context field\n// populated.\n//\n// For details on Raft membership changes, see:\n//\n// [1]: https://github.com/ongardie/dissertation/blob/master/online-trim.pdf\nmessage ConfChangeV2 {\n\toptional ConfChangeTransition transition = 1 [(gogoproto.nullable) = false];\n\trepeated ConfChangeSingle     changes =    2 [(gogoproto.nullable) = false];\n\toptional bytes                context =    3;\n}\n\t\t\tTracker:   r.trk,\n\t\t\tLastIndex: r.raftLog.lastIndex(),\n\t\t}\n\t\tif cc.LeaveJoint() {\n\t\t\treturn changer.LeaveJoint()\n\t\t} else if autoLeave, ok := cc.EnterJoint(); ok {\n\t\t\treturn changer.EnterJoint(autoLeave, cc.Changes...)\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn changer.Simple(cc.Changes...)\n\t}()\n\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\t// TODO(tbg): return the error to the caller.\n\t\tpanic(err)\n\t}\n\n\treturn r.switchToConfig(cfg, trk)\n}\n\n// switchToConfig reconfigures this node to use the provided configuration. It\n// updates the in-memory state and, when necessary, carries out additional\n// actions such as reacting to the removal of nodes or changed quorum\n// requirements.\n//\n// The inputs usually result from restoring a ConfState or applying a ConfChange.\nfunc (r *raft) switchToConfig(cfg tracker.Config, trk tracker.ProgressMap) pb.ConfState {\n\ttraceConfChangeEvent(cfg, r)\n\n\tr.trk.Config = cfg\n\tr.trk.Progress = trk\n\n\tr.logger.Infof(\"%x switched to configuration %s\", r.id, r.trk.Config)\n\tcs := r.trk.ConfState()\n\tpr, ok := r.trk.Progress[r.id]\n\n\t// Update whether the node itself is a learner, resetting to false when the\n\t// node is removed.\n\tr.isLearner = ok && pr.IsLearner\n\n\tif (!ok || r.isLearner) && r.state == StateLeader {\n\t\t// This node is leader and was removed or demoted, step down if requested.\n\t\t//\n\t\t// We prevent demotions at the time writing but hypothetically we handle\n\t\t// them the same way as removing the leader.\n\t\t//\n\t\t// TODO(tbg): ask follower with largest Match to TimeoutNow (to avoid\n\t\t// interruption). This might still drop some proposals but it's better than\n\t\t// nothing.\n\t\tif r.stepDownOnRemoval {\n\t\t\tr.becomeFollower(r.Term, None)\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn cs\n\t}\n\n\t// The remaining steps only make sense if this node is the leader and there\n\t// are other nodes.\n\tif r.state != StateLeader || len(cs.Voters) == 0 {\n\t\treturn cs\n\t}\n\n\tif r.maybeCommit() {\n\t\t// If the configuration change means that more entries are committed now,\n\t\t// broadcast/append to everyone in the updated config.\n\t\tr.bcastAppend()\n\t} else {\n\t\t// Otherwise, still probe the newly added replicas; there's no reason to\n\t\t// let them wait out a heartbeat interval (or the next incoming\n\t\t// proposal).\n\t\tr.trk.Visit(func(id uint64, _ *tracker.Progress) {\n\t\t\tif id == r.id {\n\t\t\t\treturn\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tr.maybeSendAppend(id, false /* sendIfEmpty */)\n\t\t})\n\t}\n\t// If the leadTransferee was removed or demoted, abort the leadership transfer.\n\tif _, tOK := r.trk.Config.Voters.IDs()[r.leadTransferee]; !tOK && r.leadTransferee != 0 {\n\t\tr.abortLeaderTransfer()\n\t}\n\n\treturn cs\n}\n// this when it applies a configuration change, except when it decides to reject\n// the configuration change, in which case no call must take place.\nfunc (rn *RawNode) ApplyConfChange(cc pb.ConfChangeI) *pb.ConfState {\n\tcs := rn.raft.applyConfChange(cc.AsV2())\n\treturn &cs\n}\n\n// Step advances the state machine using the given message.\nfunc (rn *RawNode) Step(m pb.Message) error {\n\t// Ignore unexpected local messages receiving over network.\n\tif IsLocalMsg(m.Type) && !IsLocalMsgTarget(m.From) {\n\t\treturn ErrStepLocalMsg\n\t}\n\tif IsResponseMsg(m.Type) && !IsLocalMsgTarget(m.From) && rn.raft.trk.Progress[m.From] == nil {\n\t\treturn ErrStepPeerNotFound\n\t}\n\treturn rn.raft.Step(m)\n}\n\n// Ready returns the outstanding work that the application needs to handle. This\n// includes appending and applying entries or a snapshot, updating the HardState,\n// and sending messages. The returned Ready() *must* be handled and subsequently\n// passed back via Advance().\nfunc (rn *RawNode) Ready() Ready {\n\trd := rn.readyWithoutAccept()\n\trn.acceptReady(rd)\n\treturn rd\n}\n\n// readyWithoutAccept returns a Ready. This is a read-only operation, i.e. there\n// is no obligation that the Ready must be handled.\nfunc (rn *RawNode) readyWithoutAccept() Ready {\n\tr := rn.raft\n\n\trd := Ready{\n\t\tEntries:          r.raftLog.nextUnstableEnts(),\n\t\tCommittedEntries: r.raftLog.nextCommittedEnts(rn.applyUnstableEntries()),\n\t\tMessages:         r.msgs,\n\t}\n\tif softSt := r.softState(); !softSt.equal(rn.prevSoftSt) {\n\t\t// Allocate only when SoftState changes.\n\t\tescapingSoftSt := softSt\n\t\trd.SoftState = &escapingSoftSt\n\t}\n              |             |   (match=m.index,next=match+1)                                    \n              |             |                                                                   \n              |             |                                                                   \n              |             |                                                                   \n              |   +---------v----------+                                                        \n              |   |     replicate      |                                                        \n              +---+  max inflight = n  |                                                        \n                  +--------------------+                                                        \n```\n\nWhen the progress of a follower is in `probe` state, leader sends at most one `replication message` per heartbeat interval. The leader sends `replication message` slowly and probing the actual progress of the follower. A `msgHeartbeatResp` or a `msgAppResp` with reject might trigger the sending of the next `replication message`.\n\nWhen the progress of a follower is in `replicate` state, leader sends `replication message`, then optimistically increases `next` to the latest entry sent. This is an optimized state for fast replicating log entries to the follower.\n\nWhen the progress of a follower is in `snapshot` state, leader stops sending any `replication message`.\n\nA newly elected leader sets the progresses of all the followers to `probe` state with `match` = 0 and `next` = last index. The leader slowly (at most once per heartbeat) sends `replication message` to the follower and probes its progress.\n\nA progress changes to `replicate` when the follower replies with a non-rejection `msgAppResp`, which implies that it has matched the index sent. At this point, leader starts to stream log entries to the follower fast. The progress will fall back to `probe` when the follower replies a rejection `msgAppResp` or the link layer reports the follower is unreachable. We aggressively reset `next` to `match`+1 since if we receive any `msgAppResp` soon, both `match` and `next` will increase directly to the `index` in `msgAppResp`. (We might end up with sending some duplicate entries when aggressively reset `next` too low.  see open question)\n\nA progress changes from `probe` to `snapshot` when the follower falls very far behind and requires a snapshot. After sending `msgSnap`, the leader waits until the success, failure or abortion of the previous snapshot sent. The progress will go back to `probe` after the sending result is applied.\n\n### Flow Control\n\n1. limit the max size of message sent per message. Max should be configurable.\nLower the cost at probing state as we limit the size per message; lower the penalty when aggressively decreased to a too low `next`\n\n2. limit the # of in flight messages < N when in `replicate` state. N should be configurable. Most implementation will have a sending buffer on top of its actual network transport layer (not blocking raft node). We want to make sure raft does not overflow that buffer, which can cause message dropping and triggering a bunch of unnecessary resending repeatedly. \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > build_config_candidate.py <<'PY'\nimport json\nm=json.load(open('../audit-spec/v4.json'));fid='F_active_configuration'\nproducers=['B_configuration','B_recovery'];consumers=['B_configuration','B_aggregate_commit','B_elect_transfer','B_read_authority','B_propose_replicate']\nfor b in m['behaviors']:\n if b['id'] in producers:b['produces_fact_ids'].append(fid)\n if b['id'] in consumers:b['consumes_fact_ids'].append(fid)\n if b['id']=='B_configuration':\n  b['reads'].append('ApplyConfChange receives an unindexed ConfChange delta and applies it to the current tracker configuration; neither RawNode entry nor Node channel wrapper conveys the committed entry index or a snapshot generation.')\n  b['source_ids']+=['S_raw_entry','S_node_conf_entry','S_node_loop','S_config_activation']\n  b['unknowns'].append('Caller responsibility for a previously issued committed configuration delta after network snapshot restore has installed a newer ConfState is not explicit in the acquired asynchronous usage contract.')\nm['facts'].append(dict(id=fid,meaning='The current tracker configuration determines local voter/learner eligibility and quorum interpretation. It is the result of the latest executed configuration delta or ConfState reconstruction at this instance; this representation alone does not certify correspondence to the latest applied log prefix.',identity={'owner':'local raft instance and current tracker','membership':'tracker.Config incoming/outgoing voters, Learners and LearnersNext'},validity_context='ApplyConfChange transforms the current configuration without a committed-entry index parameter. Full snapshot restore installs ConfState immediately; local snapshot completion changes log/application progress without reapplying the snapshot configuration. Matching-prefix fast-forward does not replace configuration.',representation=['r.trk.Config','r.trk.Progress participant roles','RawNode.Status().Config copy'],invalidators=['B_configuration','B_recovery'],reinterpreters=[],durability='Volatile active membership. Returned ConfState must be recorded in snapshots by the caller; restart reconstructs from Storage. No automatic persisted configuration-generation token is exposed by ApplyConfChange.',recovery='confchange.Restore reconstructs incoming/outgoing membership and tracker from stored or incoming snapshot ConfState.',source_ids=['S_restore_config','S_change','S_change_members','S_config_restore','S_raw_entry','S_node_conf_entry','S_node_loop','S_status_copy','S_apply_progress','S_config_activation'],unknowns=['Whether and how a caller must suppress or serialize an already issued configuration entry when a later snapshot is restored before its ApplyConfChange call.']))\nfor f in m['facts']:f.pop('established_by',None);f.pop('consumed_by',None)\nfor a in m['activities']:a.pop('behavior_ids',None)\nchanges={bid:dict(impact='dependency',source_ids=['S_restore_config','S_raw_entry','S_node_loop','S_node_conf_entry','S_config_activation'],rationale='Add explicit active-configuration production and consumption navigation; distinguish current mutable membership from guaranteed correspondence to an application prefix.',preserves='All three prior fixed obligations retain their Fact meanings, drivers and observation scopes. The quorum and campaign histories have fixed voters; the read check already observes an actual applied joint change. Adding this separate configuration representation does not reinterpret those measurements or assert a wider safety conclusion.') for bid in set(producers+consumers)}\njson.dump(m,open('configuration-lifecycle-map.json','w'),indent=2)\nq=dict(question='What caller responsibility preserves snapshot-established membership when an earlier committed configuration entry was already handed out but its ApplyConfChange runs after full snapshot restore?',importance='A stale configuration delta could change participant eligibility after recovery. The observed learner-only discrepancy concerns local membership; a voter quorum change, committed conflict or client consequence is not established.',disposition='needs_specific_evidence',preferred_check='source_review',participants=['Lagging RawNode follower','Application owning issued committed-entry work and snapshot installation','Leader producing a later configuration snapshot'],objects=['Queued committed ConfChange','Snapshot ConfState','Active tracker configuration','Application progress'],contexts=['AsyncStorageWrites, reliable FIFO local targets and sequential RawNode calls','Learner add followed by learner removal, fixed three-voter quorum','Full snapshot restore occurs before applying the older add; application still processes the old entry before installing snapshot state'],event_paths=['ProposeConfChange(add learner) -> actual commitment -> issued apply batch held','Later removal committed/applied on leader -> snapshot/compaction -> automatic MsgSnap -> restore ConfState','Older queued ApplyConfChange -> current configuration delta -> snapshot completion -> Status.Config'],activity_classes=['A4','A2','A3','A5'],behavior_ids=['B_configuration','B_recovery'],supporting_behavior_ids={'B_ready_application':'Issues committed batches and advances progress independently of snapshot receipt.','B_propose_replicate':'Produces configuration entries and automatic snapshot delivery.','B_aggregate_commit':'Establishes the leader prefix underlying the snapshot.','B_step_context_and_storage':'Snapshot completion advances progress without reinstalling configuration.'},fact_ids=[fid],obligation_relation_kind='preservation',counterevidence=['Caller may owe a barrier before stepping MsgSnap or suppression of obsolete configuration work; current acquired documentation does not explicitly resolve this.','Synchronous documentation requires Ready updates in order; asynchronous documentation replaces those fields with independent target queues. Their relation to immediate network restore needs a precise applicability interpretation.','The explored add/remove affects only a learner and cannot itself establish a voter-quorum safety violation.'],unknowns=['Identify whether the selected older ApplyConfChange call is authorized after network full restore, despite originating from an actual issued committed batch.','Compare a caller that completes that older batch before delivering MsgSnap, retaining identical producer inputs, to localize the ordering dependency.','If an applicable preservation responsibility can be sourced, fix it for fresh formal execution rather than relabeling exploratory observations.'],trigger_rationale='Exploration 71b0b0216b9f493dabbfcc7ac791b691 observed no learner 4 immediately after snapshot restore, learner 4 after the older add, and learner 4 still present after matching snapshot completion at applied/commit 7. The next useful distinction is caller ordering permission and a before-delivery control, not an expanded consensus consequence.')\nids=set(m['facts'][-1]['source_ids'])\nfor b in m['behaviors']:\n if b['id'] in q['behavior_ids'] or b['id'] in q['supporting_behavior_ids']:ids.update(b['source_ids'])\nids.update(['S_async_usage','S_ready_usage','S_test_apply','S_test_append']);q['source_ids']=sorted(ids)\ns=dict(action='continue',rationale='Retain the conditional configuration observation and map the active membership lifecycle before pursuing contract applicability.',sources=[dict(id='S_config_activation',file='raftpb/raft.proto',start_line=178,end_line=213,kind='document_statement')],map_path='configuration-lifecycle-map.json',map_changes=changes,question=q,feedback=dict(ref_ids=['71b0b0216b9f493dabbfcc7ac791b691','S_config_activation','S_raw_entry','S_node_conf_entry','S_async_usage','S_restore_config'],answered='Exploration 71b0b0216b9f493dabbfcc7ac791b691 completed the actual learner-add/removal and snapshot prefix. Snapshot index 7 excludes learner 4. Public Status.Config excl